On Wednesday, June 3, 2015 at 2:30:54 AM UTC-4, Michael Betancourt wrote:
> > I think there should be sampling() and optimizing() --- perhaps with reverbing --- that would take each take a subset of stan_config classes. To my mind, VB is optimizing and just needs a (future) stan_vb config to distinguish it from ML. Both sampling() and optimizing() could accept a stan_diagnose config since at the moment it just streams the gradient comparison, but we could do something different if we ever had algorithm-specific diagnostics. Then stan_hmc, stan_rmhmc, etc. would be valid configs for sampling(). And if Michael needs us to utilize an uber-class internally to call the C++, then fine (this detail has never struck me as a big concern).
> >
>
> I disagree — I think ever new algorithmic approach (optimization, MCMC, VB, EP, MML, diagnostics) should
> be a separate call.  VB is very much fundamentally different from just an optimization of some objective function.

But given the existence of a configuration object that uniquely defines the approach, what is the benefit to users of making them learn to call a different function whose name corresponds to part of the configuration? VB is fundamentally different from penalized ML but how is optimization with VB configuration fundamentally different from optimization with penalized ML configuration?

Ben

