Just a quick comment that I don’t think we need to insist on precise 2.5% intervals.  That’s old-style thinking, in which the goal of a statistical analysis is to check whether the 95% interval includes zero.

In practice, the interval [-1.9, 2.1] is as good as [-2, 2] or [-2, 1.9] or whatever.

> On Jun 23, 2015, at 6:41 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
>
> You need to run a lot of iterations to get good 2.5% intervals.
> And this is likely to be where we disagree with BUGS by getting
> more into tails.  And this is also why Gibbs shouldn't be the gold
> standard.  See the Betancourt and Girolami paper on hierarchical
> models.

